Forward, you inject data directly before the push — no lifecycle callback needed. Backward, the standard pattern is a closure or delegate the pushed controller calls before popping.

```swift
// Forward: set data before pushing
let detail = DetailViewController()
detail.item = selectedItem
navigationController?.pushViewController(detail, animated: true)

// Backward: closure called just before the pop
class DetailViewController: UIViewController {
    var onSave: ((Item) -> Void)?

    @objc func saveTapped() {
        onSave?(editedItem)
        navigationController?.popViewController(animated: true)
    }
}
```

The closure approach avoids the tight coupling of delegate protocols for simple one-shot callbacks, but capture `self` weakly on the caller's side if the closure is stored — otherwise you've built a retain cycle between the two controllers.
